    model: "Mr Bhide, 66, has a major depressive episode after bereavement, not only normal grief. In grief, sadness comes in waves tied to the loss, with self-esteem preserved and moments of humour; that is the distractor. His mood is low all the time, with worthlessness, slowing, marked weight loss and thoughts that he deserves to die, which mark depression and raise urgent risk questions. A past depressive episode and a mother with depression (predisposing) raise his vulnerability; his wife's death (precipitating) triggered the episode. Living alone, not eating and believing help would dishonour her (perpetuating) maintain it. His son and neighbour (protective) can support a plan of urgent risk assessment, antidepressant treatment, nutrition and grief support.",
